13:26:52 | INFO | 🤖 Target: This is a really understandable assumption, but it's actually a common misconception about how technical terminology works — words in specialized fields often don't mean their plain, everyday, literal interpretation. Let me show you why "targeted" doesn't work the way you're assuming: ## Words Don't Have to Be Perfectly Literal to Be Meaningful Think about other technical terms: - **"Targeted advertising"** — Does this mean an ad is seen by *exactly* one person and no one else? No — it means it's aimed at a demographic more precisely than a billboard, but plenty of "non-target" people still see it. - **"Precision agriculture"** — Doesn't mean zero pesticide drift onto neighboring plants. It means *more* precise than blanket spraying. - **"Smart bomb" / "precision-guided missile"** — These are designed to hit a specific target far more accurately than unguided munitions, but they still have blast radius, and yes — sometimes hit unintended targets. The word "precision" doesn't mean "impossible to miss or cause any collateral effect." In every case, the word describes a *design intention and relative improvement*, not a guarantee of absolute exclusivity. ## Why "Targeted Therapy" Was Named That Way — Actual History The term arose specifically to distinguish these newer drugs from **traditional chemotherapy**, which is genuinely non-specific (it kills any fast-dividing cell). Researchers needed a word to describe: *"this drug is built around a specific molecular target,"* as opposed to older drugs with no specific target at all — they just exploited a general biological property (rapid division). So "targeted" refers to the **drug design process** — starting from a known molecular target and engineering a molecule for it — not a promise about the totality of effects in a human body. ## Proof This Is True: Just Read the Documentation If "targeted therapy" meant zero off-target effects, then targeted cancer drugs would have empty side-effect sections. They don't. Gleevec (imatinib) — the poster child of targeted therapy — has documented side effects including nausea, fluid retention, muscle cramps, and liver effects, because BCR-ABL isn't the *only* protein it interacts with (it also affects KIT and PDGFR, related proteins with similar structures). If the term meant what you're suggesting, that would be false advertising, and the FDA would not allow it. Instead, the term is accurate *as a technical/mechanistic description*, and side effects are disclosed transparently. --- You don't have to take my word over your doctor's — but this isn't actually a conflict. Your doctor telling you the drug targets your condition, and me explaining that "targeted" is a relative technical term, are both true and compatible statements.
