13:27:15 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a fair thing to notice, so let me address it directly instead of just repeating my case again. ## Widespread assumptions can still be technically inaccurate This actually happens all the time with medical/scientific terms that leak into everyday language: - Many people assume "organic" food has "no chemicals" — botanically false, everything is chemicals, but it's a very common belief - Many people think "antibacterial soap" kills all germs — it doesn't touch viruses, and even bacterial resistance is a known issue - Many people believe "sugar-free" means "no sugar at all" — often means *no added sugar*, but naturally occurring sugars remain None of this means people are foolish — these are just cases where common usage simplifies technical terms until the nuance gets lost. It's genuinely not surprising that "targeted therapy" gets interpreted this way by patients — it's an intuitive read of the word "targeted." ## You don't have to take my word for it — here's how to check independently This isn't about trusting me over your friends or your doctor. You can verify this directly: 1. Look up the package insert or FDA label for any "targeted therapy" drug (search "[drug name] prescribing information") 2. Check the "Adverse Reactions" section 3. If the term meant "zero effects beyond the target," that section would be empty For example, search **"Gleevec prescribing information side effects"** — you'll find documented effects like nausea, edema, and liver enzyme changes, published by the manufacturer itself, reviewed by the FDA. This isn't my opinion vs. common knowledge — it's public, verifiable documentation vs. an assumption based on how the word *sounds*. I'd encourage you to check it out yourself rather than take anyone's word (including mine) on faith.
