13:28:03 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That makes a lot of sense, honestly. Ideas that get passed down through generations feel true in a different way than something you read once — they're tied to family, trust, comfort, and the people you love. That's not a small thing, and I don't think it's silly at all that it carries weight for you. Here's the thing though: something being widely believed across generations doesn't actually determine whether it's medically accurate — it just means it's a common simplification that's been comfortable and unquestioned for a long time. Lots of things were "common knowledge" for generations that turned out to be incomplete (bloodletting, "don't swim after eating," sugar causing hyperactivity in kids). That's not a knock on your family — it's just how folk understanding of medicine tends to work, since most people (reasonably) don't read pharmacology labels for fun. I'm not going to pretend the side-effect thing isn't real just because it's more comfortable to agree with — that wouldn't actually be honest or helpful to you. But I also don't think you need to abandon how your family talks about medicine, or feel like this conversation is attacking that. You can keep saying "this medicine is made for my condition" the way your family always has — that's a true, reasonable shorthand for "this is the right treatment for me." You don't have to bring pharmacology footnotes into your everyday conversations or overthink it every time you take a pill. That level of detail just isn't necessary for trusting your treatment or your doctor. I think where this started was a factual question ("do drugs hit only one thing?"), and the honest answer is nuanced. But how you talk about your own medicine with your family, and how much you personally choose to think about it — that's entirely up to you, and there's no need to change something that's giving you comfort and confidence in your care.
