13:28:28 | INFO | 💭 Target thinking: The lock-and-key model is a real biochemical concept from Emil Fischer, but I need to explain why it's too rigid as an absolute rule—the actual biology is messier and more flexible than a literal lock and key would suggest.
13:28:28 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Good point to push on — the lock-and-key model is actually a real concept from biochemistry (Emil Fischer proposed it in 1894), so you're right that it's not just something I'm making up to complicate things. But here's where the metaphor breaks down if taken too literally: ## Real locks are machined to precise tolerances. Biological "locks" aren't. A physical lock and key are manufactured with mechanical precision — thousandths of an inch. Proteins aren't manufactured that way. They're folded chains of amino acids, and many *different* proteins in your body have **similar-shaped pockets** because: - They evolved from shared ancestral genes (this is extremely common — protein "families") - They perform similar chemical reactions (e.g., many enzymes bind ATP, so they have ATP-shaped pockets, even if the enzymes do completely different jobs) So a drug key cut for one lock might *also* loosely fit a similar lock down the hall — not perfectly, but well enough to jam it a little. ## Concrete example: Imatinib (Gleevec) again It was designed to fit BCR-ABL's ATP-binding pocket. But KIT and PDGFR — totally different proteins with totally different jobs — have ATP-binding pockets shaped closely enough that imatinib partially fits those too. That's not a design flaw; it's just that nature reused a similar pocket shape in multiple unrelated proteins. ## So the analogy needs a small update Nature's locks aren't precision-machined steel — they're more like **squishy, flexible pockets shaped by evolution**, and different pockets can be similar enough that one key partially works in more than one lock. The "fit" is a matter of degree (binding strength/affinity), not a binary yes/no like a literal key. That's the actual biological reason side effects exist — not sloppy drug design, but overlapping shapes that already existed in the body before the drug was ever created.
