13:29:26 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You're right to push back — I said that badly, and "sloppy guesswork" is not what I meant or what's true. Let me correct that clearly. ## What I should have said There's a difference between **how a drug was originally discovered** and **how rigorously it was tested and proven to work**. These are separate things: - **Discovery method:** Some older drugs were found through trial-and-error, natural sources, or observing effects before knowing the exact mechanism (this is true of penicillin, aspirin, many others) - **Validation/rigor:** Every drug on the market — old or new — went through the same rigorous process: chemistry, animal studies, then multiple phases of human clinical trials, statistical proof of safety and effectiveness, and ongoing FDA monitoring So "discovered by observing effects" is **not the same as** "poorly tested" or "not real science." Aspirin has been studied in thousands of trials for over a century. Ibuprofen's mechanism (COX inhibition) is extremely well understood today, even though it wasn't when first developed. These are *legitimate, rigorously proven, effective medicines* — full stop. ## Why they work great for what they're meant for This part is genuinely true and not in conflict with anything I said: these drugs are highly effective for their intended use *because* they've been extensively tested to prove exactly that — at the right dose, for the right condition, the benefit clearly and reliably outweighs the risk. That's real science, real evidence, real effectiveness. ## The one distinction that still stands The only point I was actually trying to make: knowing *how* ibuprofen works now (COX enzymes, including ones in the stomach) explains *why* it can cause stomach issues in some people with heavy/long-term use — not because it's a bad drug, but because that enzyme has more than one job in the body. That's it. That's the whole point, and it doesn't take away from the drug being well-designed, well-tested, or right for you. I overcomplicated this earlier by piling on repetitive caveats and now by using a poor word ("guesswork") that unfairly undersold these medicines. Your instinct that "these drugs clearly work great" is correct, and it doesn't contradict anything true about pharmacology.
